1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,952
Is sine of thirty plus sixty degrees
the same as sine thirty plus sine

2
00:00:04,952 --> 00:00:05,852
sixty?

3
00:00:05,978 --> 00:00:10,041
Thirty plus sixty is ninety,
and sine ninety is exactly one.

4
00:00:10,491 --> 00:00:14,704
But sine thirty plus sine sixty
is one point three six six.

5
00:00:15,154 --> 00:00:18,005
Aarav checked it twice
on his ride to college.

6
00:00:18,355 --> 00:00:20,613
So how do we find the sine of a sum?

7
00:00:21,263 --> 00:00:26,610
A compound angle is a sum or difference
of two angles, like x plus y, or

8
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:27,643
x minus y.

9
00:00:28,043 --> 00:00:31,906
Stack the height for thirty degrees
on top of the one for sixty.

10
00:00:32,306 --> 00:00:35,514
It pokes out above the circle,
higher than the radius.

11
00:00:35,514 --> 00:00:38,573
So the sine of a sum is
not the sum of the sines.

12
00:00:39,173 --> 00:00:40,574
Pause and think.

13
00:00:40,574 --> 00:00:45,959
Is cosine of sixty minus thirty equal
to cosine sixty minus cosine thirty?

14
00:00:49,159 --> 00:00:50,059
No!

15
00:00:49,626 --> 00:00:54,725
Cosine thirty is about zero point eight
seven, but the difference is negative.

16
00:00:55,375 --> 00:00:57,028
We need a real formula.

17
00:00:57,028 --> 00:01:00,032
Here is the NCERT proof,
on the unit circle.

18
00:01:00,382 --> 00:01:01,871
Mark four points.

19
00:01:01,871 --> 00:01:05,557
P one at angle x, P two at angle x plus y.

20
00:01:05,957 --> 00:01:11,274
P three at angle minus y, and P four
at angle zero, the point one comma

21
00:01:11,274 --> 00:01:12,174
zero.

22
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:16,258
Now turn the chord from P three
to P one, through the angle y.

23
00:01:16,658 --> 00:01:19,706
P three lands on P four, P one on P two.

24
00:01:19,706 --> 00:01:24,303
Both chords stand on the same
angle, x plus y, so they are equal.

25
00:01:24,903 --> 00:01:27,313
Now in coordinates, in four steps.

26
00:01:27,663 --> 00:01:28,563
Step one.

27
00:01:28,515 --> 00:01:32,624
Distance squared is gap across
squared, plus gap up squared.

28
00:01:32,624 --> 00:01:33,524
Pythagoras.

29
00:01:34,269 --> 00:01:35,252
Step two.

30
00:01:35,252 --> 00:01:37,495
P one is cos x, sin x.

31
00:01:37,495 --> 00:01:40,289
P three is cos y, minus sin y.

32
00:01:40,289 --> 00:01:45,793
Expand, use sin squared plus cos
squared equals one, and P one P three

33
00:01:45,793 --> 00:01:51,009
squared is two minus two, times
cos x cos y minus sin x sin y.

34
00:01:51,759 --> 00:01:52,826
Step three.

35
00:01:52,826 --> 00:01:58,673
P two is cos of x plus y, sin of
x plus y, and P four is one, zero.

36
00:01:58,673 --> 00:02:03,313
So P two P four squared is
two minus two cos of x plus y.

37
00:02:04,063 --> 00:02:05,147
Step four.

38
00:02:05,147 --> 00:02:07,627
Equal chords, equal answers.

39
00:02:07,627 --> 00:02:13,770
Cancel the twos: cos of x plus y
equals cos x cos y minus sin x sin y.

40
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:16,396
Put minus y for y.

41
00:02:16,396 --> 00:02:21,906
Cos of minus y is cos y, and
sin of minus y is minus sin y.

42
00:02:21,906 --> 00:02:26,834
So cos of x minus y is cos
x cos y plus sin x sin y.

43
00:02:27,584 --> 00:02:33,276
For sine, use one fact: sine of an
angle is cosine of ninety degrees minus

44
00:02:33,276 --> 00:02:34,176
it.

45
00:02:33,815 --> 00:02:39,695
Then sin of x plus y is sin x cos y
plus cos x sin y, and sin of x minus

46
00:02:39,695 --> 00:02:40,716
y follows.

47
00:02:41,466 --> 00:02:44,808
And tan of x plus y is sine over cosine.

48
00:02:44,808 --> 00:02:50,401
Divide top and bottom by cos x cos
y: tan x plus tan y, over one minus

49
00:02:50,401 --> 00:02:51,680
tan x tan y.

50
00:02:52,430 --> 00:02:54,111
Move x and y anywhere.

51
00:02:54,111 --> 00:02:58,198
The formula always matches; the
sum of the sines never does.

52
00:02:58,848 --> 00:02:59,989
Side by side.

53
00:02:59,989 --> 00:03:03,792
Sine mixes: sin cos plus
cos sin, keeping the sign.

54
00:03:04,192 --> 00:03:08,235
Cosine pairs: cos cos minus
sin sin, flipping the sign.

55
00:03:08,835 --> 00:03:09,848
Quick check.

56
00:03:09,848 --> 00:03:13,848
In cos of x minus y, is the
middle sign plus or minus?

57
00:03:13,848 --> 00:03:15,133
Pause and think.

58
00:03:18,333 --> 00:03:19,233
Plus!

59
00:03:18,950 --> 00:03:20,306
Cosine flips it.

60
00:03:20,956 --> 00:03:22,055
Remember this.

61
00:03:22,055 --> 00:03:24,223
Sine mixes and keeps the sign.

62
00:03:24,223 --> 00:03:26,124
Cosine pairs and flips it.

63
00:03:26,824 --> 00:03:30,957
One worked example: the exact
value of sine fifteen degrees.

64
00:03:31,307 --> 00:03:32,207
Step one.

65
00:03:32,147 --> 00:03:35,935
Fifteen is forty five minus
thirty, two angles we know.

66
00:03:36,685 --> 00:03:37,746
Step two.

67
00:03:37,746 --> 00:03:42,727
Use sin of x minus y: sin
x cos y minus cos x sin y.

68
00:03:43,477 --> 00:03:44,456
Step three.

69
00:03:44,456 --> 00:03:49,515
Root two over two times root three
over two, minus root two over two times

70
00:03:49,515 --> 00:03:50,415
a half.

71
00:03:51,032 --> 00:03:51,940
Step four.

72
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:55,113
That is root six minus
root two, over four.

73
00:03:55,863 --> 00:03:58,281
About zero point two five nine.

74
00:03:58,281 --> 00:03:59,881
A calculator agrees.

75
00:04:00,531 --> 00:04:01,997
Now, the exam trap.

76
00:04:02,347 --> 00:04:06,446
Many students put a plus sign in
the middle of cos of x plus y.

77
00:04:06,846 --> 00:04:08,755
But cosine flips the sign.

78
00:04:08,755 --> 00:04:13,493
At thirty and sixty, cos ninety is
zero, and only the minus sign gives

79
00:04:13,493 --> 00:04:14,393
zero.

80
00:04:14,721 --> 00:04:16,355
Where is this asked?

81
00:04:16,355 --> 00:04:21,653
JEE Main, as in April twenty nineteen,
and the PUC boards: exact values,

82
00:04:21,653 --> 00:04:25,952
or tan two alpha from alpha
plus beta and alpha minus beta.

83
00:04:26,552 --> 00:04:27,816
Exam level now.

84
00:04:27,816 --> 00:04:31,673
Alpha plus beta is one hundred
and thirty five degrees.

85
00:04:31,673 --> 00:04:33,098
Find this product.

86
00:04:33,098 --> 00:04:34,416
Pause and solve.

87
00:04:39,816 --> 00:04:41,076
The answer is C.

88
00:04:41,076 --> 00:04:41,976
Two.

89
00:04:42,075 --> 00:04:44,424
Tan one thirty five is minus one.

90
00:04:44,924 --> 00:04:49,321
So the sum of the tans, over one
minus their product, is minus one.

91
00:04:49,821 --> 00:04:53,488
Cross multiply: the sum is
minus one, plus the product.

92
00:04:53,988 --> 00:04:56,954
Expand: one, minus the
sum, plus the product.

93
00:04:56,954 --> 00:04:58,953
That leaves one plus one: two.

94
00:04:59,553 --> 00:05:01,015
Here is your recap.

95
00:05:01,015 --> 00:05:02,518
Screenshot this card.

96
00:05:05,718 --> 00:05:08,716
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

97
00:05:09,116 --> 00:05:10,016
One.

98
00:05:09,728 --> 00:05:13,785
Why is sine of x plus y
not sine x plus sine y?

99
00:05:14,685 --> 00:05:15,585
Two.

100
00:05:15,313 --> 00:05:18,689
Write the formula for cos of x minus y.

101
00:05:19,589 --> 00:05:20,489
Three.

102
00:05:20,221 --> 00:05:23,741
Use forty five and thirty
to find cos seventy five.

103
00:05:24,841 --> 00:05:28,666
Next time: double the angle,
and the sine does not double.

104
00:05:28,666 --> 00:05:31,204
Double angle and triple angle identities.
